  {"source":"ma_prog","target":"pac_prog","label":"algorithmic PAC denial-rate liability dispute","type":"tension","tension_flag":true,"tension_note":"MA plans' algorithmic post-acute-care denial tools (e.g. one major insurer's denial rate rose from 10.9% in 2020 to 22.7% in 2022 per a 2024 Senate report) face oversight scrutiny over care-access harm, while plans defend the tools as medical-necessity cost controls — liability for algorithm-driven denials remains unresolved."},
  {"source":"fda_dhcoe","target":"modelop","label":"regulatory clarity gap for continuously-learning AI","type":"tension","tension_flag":true,"tension_note":"FDA's PCCP framework (finalized Aug 2025) allows pre-authorized model updates but doesn't fully resolve continuous-learning oversight, while CHAI-certified assurance providers like ModelOp push a recurring-validation model — the two sides disagree on where post-market monitoring authority should sit."},
  {"source":"chai","target":"ma_prog","label":"AI-enabled tool reimbursement pathway","type":"tension","tension_flag":true,"tension_note":"CHAI-certified AI tools generate cost/quality claims but currently lack a defined CMS reimbursement pathway distinct from existing fee-for-service or MA supplemental-benefit mechanisms — vendors and CMS have not converged on how AI-enabled care should be paid for."},
  {"source":"aha","target":"team_prog","label":"mandatory-vs-voluntary dispute","type":"tension","tension_flag":true,"tension_note":"CMS designed TEAM as mandatory for selected hospitals to ensure adequate episode volume and generalizable savings evidence, while AHA argues mandatory participation imposes undue financial risk on hospitals with limited care-redesign capacity — the mandatory-vs-voluntary structure remains actively contested heading into the 2026 performance year.","relationship_type":"direct"}
